{"records":[{"id":"jmb4bdxc47","title":"CD168+ macrophages promote hepatocellular carcinoma tumor stemness and progression through TOP2A/β-catenin/YAP1 axis","description":"CD168+ macrophages promote hepatocellular carcinoma tumor stemness and progression through TOP2A/β-catenin/YAP1 axis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-21T07:19:04.731Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jmb4bdxc47","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jmb4bdxc47"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"changzhou","last_name":"chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biographical Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3wxt7kzvpb","title":"A key role for the exoribonuclease XRN1 in regulating the  hepatitis B viral transcriptome.","description":"Chronic hepatitis B virus (HBV) is a leading cause of liver disease and hepatocellular carcinoma that persists as a DNA mini-chromosome and replicates via genomic RNA intermediates. While HBV replication has been extensively studied, mechanisms governing viral RNA degradation remain unclear. Here, we identify a key role for the 5′-3′ exoribonuclease XRN1 in regulating the HBV transcriptome. Pharmacologic inhibition or genetic ablation of XRN1 increased HBV subgenomic RNAs without affecting pregenomic RNA (pgRNA) levels. Long-read RNA-seq showed that subgenomic RNAs, but not pgRNA, are degraded by XRN1, consistent with differential 5′ cap modification and processing (P-) body localisation. HBV infection increased P-body number and size, and delivery of synthetic pgRNA demonstrated that active replication, not RNA expression alone, alters the abundance of P-bodies. These findings identify XRN1 as a key regulator of HBV RNA turnover and uncover a mechanism for viral replication to remodel host RNA dynamics. \nThis archive contains the original uncropped images and western blots used in the manuscript, together with the numerical values used in the graphs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-21T00:42:11.598Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3wxt7kzvpb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3wxt7kzvpb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Senko","last_name":"Tsukuda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA","Real-Time Polymerase Chain Reaction","Hepatitis B Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx8g373hpt","title":"Halting hepatocellular carcinoma: identifying intercellular crosstalk in HBV-driven disease","description":"The original experimental data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-17T16:00:33.811Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx8g373hpt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx8g373hpt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lingyun","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nzzhcypcyr","title":"Incidence and risk factors for hypoglycemia in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma：A cross-sectional study","description":"This data investigates the occurrence of hypoglycemia  with hepatocellular carcinoma in patients to obtain the incidence of hypoglycemia in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma, and through the study of related influencing factors, finds out the relevant factors and independent risks that lead to hypoglycemia in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma factor.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-01T14:26:13.125Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nzzhcypcyr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nzzhcypcyr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiumei","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaojing ","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Honghong","last_name":"Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bin","last_name":"Gan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoting","last_name":"Qiu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jie","last_name":"Qiu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jing","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoming","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cuijuan","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hepatocellular Carcinoma","Hypoglycemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"67jdvhw2tf","title":"Non-terminally exhausted tumor-resident memory HBV-specific T cell responses correlate with relapse-free survival in hepatocellular carcinoma","description":"Human lymphocytes were isolated from 46 HCC patients' PBMC, healthy liver margin (HM) and HCC liver tumor (HCC), and probed by a total of 1169 T cell epitopes directly ex vivo using highly multiplexed combinatorial peptide-MHC tetramers strategy and mass cytometry. The pMHC tetramers (restricted to HLA-A*02:01, HLA-A*24:02, HLA-A*11:01 and HLA-B*58:01) library includes 258 HBV, 499 tumor-associated (TA) and 90 bystander virus epitopes, as well as 322 tumor mutation-derived neoantigens. The tetramer positive events were automatically identified and validated by tetramer deconvolution algorithm, and further evaluated manually with the frequency of antigen-specific T cells > 0.002% of total CD8 T cells and a minimum of 4 events (with 2 exceptions that there were 3 events but very strong tetramer signal). All pMHC monomer and the metal-conjugated streptavidin proteins were made in-house. The experiment was performed by using the CyTOF Helios system. This dataset has total of 374 FCS files, including all the detected antigen-specific (241 FCS files) and tetramer negative global (133 FCS files) CD8 T cells. Detailed information can be found in the manuscript and the Supplementary Information and Tables in Cheng et al., 2021, Immunity.\n\nFCS file annotation = PatientID-Tissue-Source virus/TA protein-epitope location:\nTissue: hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), healthy liver margin (HM), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC), healthy liver (HL), healthy fetal liver (HFL).\nHepatitis B virus (HBV): HBV surface (envelope) protein (HBV-S), HBV core protein (HBV-C), HBV x protein (HBV-X), HBV polymerase protein (HBV-P)\nTumor-associated antigen (TA): SSX2, GPC3, SCCA-1, AFP, TERT, NY-ESO-1, MAGE-1 (not detected), MAGE-3 (not detected)\nNeoantigen: MT_1\nBystander virus: Epstein–Barr virus (EBV), cytomegalovirus (CMV), influenza A virus (IAV), dengue virus (DENV)\nGlobal (tetramer negative) CD8 T cells = CD8TetN\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-16T08:24:30.937Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/67jdvhw2tf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/67jdvhw2tf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":"0000-0002-5906-7694"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6djr385tf","title":"Apoptotic vesicles rebalance glycolytic metabolism to boost anti-PD-1 therapy for liver cancer","description":"TCGA datasets of 371 hepatocellular carcinoma analysis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-27T20:09:12.819Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6djr385tf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6djr385tf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"jinyang","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gz2fgbkw4c","title":"fatty_DM","description":"MetabMetabolic Risk Factors and Hepatocellular Carcinoma in Fatty Liver Disease","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-03T16:52:05.68Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gz2fgbkw4c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["1